<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Borromini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1599">1599</a>–<a href="/years/1667">1667</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Francesco Castelli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Borromini, born Francesco Castelli at Bissone in today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>, was an Italian architect who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Gian Lorenzo Bernini</a> and Pietro da Cortona, was a leading figure of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architecture. The son of a stonemason, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1619 to work for his relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-maderno">Carlo Maderno</a> at St. Peter's and the Palazzo Barberini. In 1634 he received his first major commission, San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane, and later works include Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza and Sant'Agnese in Agone. His buildings manipulated Classical forms with geometrical plans and symbolic meanings. Melancholic and quick-tempered, he died by suicide in 1667.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-borromini.jpg" alt="Francesco Borromini" width="310" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borromini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
